                               SIMPLE
                       Introductory Overview
                         Guy L. Steele Jr.
                          9 November 1978

	For my project I am designing a small integrated
micro-processor for LISP expressions.  Because no computer science
project is complete without an acronym, I will refer to this project
as the "Small Integrated Micro-Processor for Lisp Expressions", or
"SIMPLE".

	SIMPLE is essentially a pointer-shuffler.  Like most simple
microprocessors, it is to be connected to a single memory which
holds both the program to be interpreted by the processor and the
data on which the interpreted program is to operate.  Unlike most
microprocessors, SIMPLE does not treat its memory as a linear array
of words of bits.  In particular, the linearity of memory has
nothing to do with the organization of the interpreted code; there
is no linearly advancing "program counter".  Instead, each memory
word contains two quantities, a "type" and a "pointer".  Normally a
pointer is a datum which, when fed to the memory system, specifies
one or more words to be retrieved or written into.  Thus a pointer
is an address; but the fact that addresses can be treated as numbers
for arithmetic purposes is used only to a very limited extent.  It
is more appropriate to think of a pointer as simply a symbol with
which some words are associated.  The type accompanying a pointer
determines how many words are associated with the pointer, and their
intended interpretation.  For some types, the pointer is not an
address at all, but some kind of "immediate data", such as a number
or a character.

	One interesting type is the "list cell".  A pointer with
type "list" is associated with two words, called the CAR and the CDR
of the list cell.  Each of these words in turn contains a
type/pointer pair, and so may point to other list cells.  Using
these list cells we can construct binary trees in the usual LISP
manner.  By thinking of CAR pointers as "downward" and CDR pointers
as "rightward", we can, again in the usual LISP manner, construct
n-ary trees, using CDR pointers to chain the pointers from a single
node, and CAR pointers to point to sons of the node.

	The "code" interpreted by the microprocessor is in the form
of such a tree.  The pointer to such a tree will have a type, of
course.  However, in this context the type is interpreted not simply
as a storage type but as an operation code.  This operation may
require certain arguments.  If so, these arguments are calculated by
recursively evaluating subnodes of the node, according to their
respective operation codes.  Typical operation codes include "refer
to variable", "conditional", "function call", and "immediate
constant value".

	The recursive evaluation process requires the use of a stack.
This stack is maintained as a linked list in memory.

                              SIMPLE
                    External Pin Specifications
                         Guy L. Steele Jr.
                          9 November 1978

	SIMPLE is expected to be connected to a memory system
providing 1K (1024) thirteen-bit words.  Each word is divided into
three type bits T0-T2 and ten address bits A0-A9.  SIMPLE
communicates with the outside world via thirty-seven pins:

(13) IT0-IT2, IA0-IA9  (input)
	Input data from the memory system.
(13) OT0-OT2, OA0-OA9  (output)
	Output data to the memory system.  Addresses and write data
	are multiplexed on OA0-OA9 according to the ADR and WRITE
	pins.  (The output pads actually contain this information
	only if the probe controls are zero -- see below.)
(2)  ADR, WRITE  (output)
	When ADR is high, the outputs OA0-OA9 contain an address
	for the memory system; in this case OT0-OT2 are irrelevant.
	When WRITE is high, the outputs OT0-OT2, OA0-OA9 contain write
	data for the memory system.  In either case, the outputs are
	maintained for a short time after the control signal goes
	from high to low to permit proper latching of the data.
	When both lines are low, the memory system is expected to
	be presenting to the input pins IT0-IT2, IA0-IA9 the memory
	data for the address last latched.  SIMPLE never raises
	both ADR and WRITE simultaneously.
(3)  PC0-PC2  (input)
	These signals are for testing only, and are normally tied
	to ground.  The output pins normally contain addresses or
	write data for the memory system.  If the probe controls
	PC0-PC2 are non-zero, then various signals internal to the
	chip are gated onto the output pins instead.
	(N.B.  The drawings show five pins PC1-PC5, which are
	individual signals.  Layout constraints seem to indicate
	that the number of pads should be reduced to save area on the
	chip.  Hence, I here assume that I can reduce the five pads
	to three by using a binary encoding of the probe signals.)
(1)  STUFF  (input)
	This signal is for testing only, and is normally tied to
	ground.  If this signal is high, the state bits which
	serve as the "micro-PC's" for the internal control FSMs
	are forcibly loaded from the input pins IT0-IT2, IA0-IA9.
	In this way the FSMs can be forced into any given state.
	(This facility is also used to initialize the chip; for
	this purpose the input pins should all be zero.)
(1)  FREEZE  (input)
	This signal is for testing only, and is normally tied to ground.
	If this signal is high, the FSMs which control the chip
	recirculate in the same state instead of advancing.  This
	signal defers to STUFF.
(2)  PHI1, PHI2  (input)
	Two-phase clock signals.
(2)  VDD, GND  (input)
	Power supply lines.
(37) pins total.

                              SIMPLE
                         "Instruction Set"
                         Guy L. Steele Jr.
                          9 November 1978

	The "instruction set" processed by SIMPLE is actually a
modified version of SCHEME, a dialect of LISP.  The tree-like
expressions are essentially LISP S-expressions which constitute a
slightly "compiled" version of the usual LISP code.  The main effect
of this compilation is to pre-calculate the positions of variables
in the environment so that variable references do not require
search.  This in turn simplifies the structure of the environment
and of procedures.

	When the chip is initialized, it takes a given expression
(how it is given is described elsewhere), and uses a null
environment to evaluate it.  If that evaluation ever terminates, the
chip drops dead.  It also drops dead if it runs out of memory.

	When the chip is asked to evaluate an expression, it
examines the type field.  This provides eight "op codes":
	0 = constant list		4 = procedure
	1 = constant symbol		5 = conditional (if-then-else)
	2 = variable reference		6 = procedure call
	3 = constant closure		7 = quoted constant The
address part of the word has different purposes depending on the
type.  For type 2, it is the negative of the position in the
environment of the variable to be referenced.  For types 0, 1, 3, 5,
and 6, the address points to the first of two consecutive words;
the first is the CDR, and the second the CAR.  For types 4 and 7,
the address points to a single word, referred to as the "CDR" for
compatibility with the previous case.

	The evaluation of type 0 (list), 1 (symbol), or 3 (closure)
simply results in that object; such objects are "self-evaluating".
(Notice that symbols are not the same as variables here; this usage
has been "compiled out".  The only purpose for symbols here is that
they are atomic, as opposed to lists, which are not.)

	The evaluation of type 7 (quote) returns the CDR of the
object.  In this way any object whatsoever, not just a list, symbol,
or closure, can be included as a constant datum in a program.

	The evaluation of type 2 (variable reference) chains (CDRs)
down the environment one less than the negative of the number in the
address part of the expression pointer.  It then takes the CAR, and
returns the value so found.

	The evaluation of a procedure (type 4) results in a pointer
to a newly-allocated word pair.  This pointer has type 3 (closure).
The CAR of the pair contains the CDR of the procedure; this is the
code body of the procedure.  The CDR of the pair contains the
current environment.  In this way the code and the encironment are
bound up together (as a "FUNARG") for later application.

	A conditional (type 5) is a "3-list"; that is, it points to
a cell whose CDR points to another cell whose CDR points to another
cell whose CDR should be NIL (the zero type/zero address pointer).
The successive CARs in this list are respectively a predicate
expression (IF), a consequent expression (THEN), and an alternative
expression (ELSE).  The predicate expression is evaluated first;
depending on whether the result is non-NIL or NIL, then the
consequent or alternative is evaluated, throwing away the other one,
to produce the value of the conditional.

	A procedure call (type 6) is the most complicated of the
lot.  It is a list of indefinite length, chained together by CDR
pointers.  Each CDR pointer except the last MUST have type 0 (list).
The last CDR pointer should have a zero address and a NON-zero type.
This last type specifies the operation to be performed.  In CDRing
down the list, SIMPLE evaluates each of the expressions in the CAR,
saving the resulting values.  These values are available as
arguments to the operation to be performed.  The operations
available are:
	0 = <more arguments>		4 = EQ
	1 = CAR				5 = ATOM
	2 = CDR				6 = LIST
	3 = CONS			7 = FUNCALL
For operations CAR, CDR, and ATOM there should be one argument;
for CONS and EQ, two.  No checking is performed for this.

	CAR, CDR, CONS, EQ, ATOM, and LIST are the standard LISP
primitive operations.  FUNCALL is the operation which calls a user
procedure (actually a closure).  The LAST argument (NOT the first!)
MUST be a closure (this is not checked for!), which is applied to
the preceding arguments.  (NO checking is performed to see whether
the correct number of arguments has been supplied!) The body (CAR)
of the closure is evaluated in an environment produced by tacking
(NCONCing) ALL the arguments onto the front of the environment (CDR)
of the closure.  In this way "lexical scoping" is achieved as in
SCHEME or ALGOL.  Notice that the closure itself is added to the
environment along with the other arguments.  In this way the
procedure can refer to itself recursively.

	As an example, an expression calling for the evaluation
of (APPEND '(A B C) '(D E F)), including the definition of APPEND,
is shown in an accompanying diagram.

                              SIMPLE
                          Initialization
                         Guy L. Steele Jr.
                          9 November 1978

	The chip is initialized by holding all input pins IT0-IT2,
IA0-IA9 low while raising STUFF, and stepping the clock for a few
cycles.  Then STUFF should be lowered.  Whenever FREEZE is also low
during a clock cycle, the chip will run.

	The initial contents of memory should be as follows:
Location	Contents	Remarks
   0		   0|0		CDR part of NIL (points to NIL)
   1		   0|0		CAR part of NIL (points to NIL)
   2		   xxx		CDR part of T
   3		   xxx		CAR part of T
   4		   0|p		Beginning of free storage
   5		   exp		Expression to be evaluated

	NIL must be at location 0, and T at location 2.  These are
used as returned values by the built-in predicates EQ and ATOM.
Notice that NIL is considered to be a list.  Location 5 is the
expression to be evaluated.  Additional cells in the expression may
occupy succeeding words as appropriate.  Location 4 should point at
the last word used in the initial expression.  Words after that
pointed to by location 4 are assumed to be free for allocation by
the "garbage collector".  (Actually, SIMPLE has no garbage
collector.  When storage has been once allocated, it drops dead.)
The actual free storage pointer is not maintained in location 4; it
is not changed.  It is only used to initialize the free storage
pointer internal to the chip.

                              SIMPLE
                  Register/Logic Level Description
                         Guy L. Steele Jr.
                          9 November 1978

	Internally SIMPLE is organized into two parts.  One part,
EVAL, is concerned with the evaluation algorithm.  The other part is
concerned with storage management, and is called GC.  One way to
think about this division is that GC supplies a set of "subroutines"
which are used by EVAL to deal with the memory system.  In this way
EVAL can deal with the details of the evaluation of the code in
terms of a linked-list memory system.  GC implements this memory
system in terms of the usual "linear-array" memory system.

	Each of the two parts, EVAL and GC, is itself divided into
two parts: registers and control.  The registers provide storage for
type/pointer words, and are connected by a common bus in each part.
The control is a finite-state machine implemented as a PLA.  Each
PLA is organized as a micro-code ROM, addressed by a "micro-PC" and
yielding a set of control signals, including register controls and a
new micro-PC indicating the next state.

	EVAL has five registers, called V (Value), X (Expression), N
(Environment), L (List of arguments), and C (Control stack).  Each
can hold a thirteen-bit word of three type bits T0-T2 and ten
address bits A0-A9.  (These bits are therefore referred to as VT0,
VT1, XA0, NA6, etc.)  They are connected together by a common bus
called the E bus.  Any of these registers can be loaded from the bus
(according to the signals LDV, LDX, LDN, LDL, LDC) or read from the
bus (according to the signals RDV, RDX, RDN, RDL, RDC).  In
addition, register X has incrementation logic associated with it.
The signal RDX+ causes the contents of X, plus 1, to be read onto the
E bus.  (This incrementer constitutes the "arithmetic" portion of
EVAL's ALU.)  The type bits do not participate in the incrementation,
only the address bits.  (If the address bits of X are all ones,
then incrementing X reads an all-zero address part, but does not
read an incremented type.)

	GC has three-and-a-half registers, called P, Q, R, and S.
They are connected by a common bus called the G bus.
They can be loaded from the G bus (according to the signals LDP,
LDQ, LDR, LDS) and read back onto the G bus (according to the signals
RDP, RDQ, RDR).  Register S cannot be read back onto the G bus.
The output pads O are ordinarily driven from the contents of
register S; thus register S serves as the latch for the output pads.
Register P, like register X, has associated incrementation logic;
the contents of P, plus 1, can be read onto the G bus according
to the signal RDP+.  Register P is also special (and unlike X)
in that its type bits are always zero.  Reading register P always
forces zeroes onto GT0-GT2, and loading P always discards the
type bits.

	Here is a complete list of the signals produced by the
two finite-state machines EVAL and GC:

EVAL	Signal name			Remarks
    7	ENSTATE0-ENSTATE6		Next state
    7	EOSTATE0-EOSTATE6		Old (current) state
    2	ET DISP, EAZ/ETZ/EQV DISP	Dispatch selectors
    5	LDV, LDX, LDN, LDL, LDC		Register load controls
    6	RDV, RDX, RDX+, RDN, RDL, RDC	Register read controls
    4	GCOP0-GCOP3			GC OPeration request
    3	LIT0-LIT2			Lieral value
    2	EA LIT, ET LIT			Literal read controls
Total 36 signals

GC	Signal name			Remarks
    6	GNSTATE0-GNSTATE6		Next state
    6	GOSTATE0-GOSTATE6		Old (current) state
    2	GCOP DISP, GA ZERO DISP		Dispatch selectors
    4	LDP, LDQ, LDR, LDS		Register load controls
    4	RDP, RDP+, RDQ, RDR		Register read controls
    3	CONN I, CONN EA, CONN ET	Internal bus connections
    1	STEP EVAL			Advance EVAL FSM
    2	ADR, WRITE			Memory controls
Total 28 signals

	The two finite state machines are clocked in parallel.  At
each step a new state is latched for each FSM.  This new state can
come from one of three sources: the old state (OSTATE), the next
state (NSTATE), or the input pads (I).  It comes from I if STUFF is
high.  It comes from OSTATE if STUFF is low and FREEZE is high.
Otherwise, it comes from NSTATE.  The preceding contains two lies.
One is that the EVAL FSM actually uses OSTATE if either FREEZE is
high OR if STEP EVAL is not asserted by the GC FSM; in this way GC
controls the advance of EVAL.

	The other lie is that each FSM has two dispatch control
lines.  If either one is asserted (it should never occur that both
are asserted), then certain control signals are OR'd with the low
bits of NSTATE to produce the new state (if so selected by STUFF and
FREEZE).  This latter facility provides for conditional dispatching
in the "microcode" for the FSMs:  if the microword provides
zero-bits in the low bits of NSTATE, then the next microinstruction
is selected according to the control signals specified.  Some of the
control signals may be effectively masked by specifying one-bits in
NSTATE corresponding to those signals.  The control signals which
may be dispatched on, and their corresponding dispatch selectors,
are:

FSM	Selector		Signals
EVAL	ET DISP			ET0-ET2		(8-way dispatch)
EVAL	EAZ/ETZ/EQV DISP 	EA ZERO, ET ZERO, EQV	(8-way)
GC	GCOP DISP		GCOP0-GCOP3		(16-way)
GC	GA ZERO DISP		GA ZERO			(2-way)

	EA ZERO is asserted if the address bits on the E bus are all
zero.  ET ZERO similarly is asserted if the type bits on the E bus
are all zero.  EQV is asserted if all the E bus bits match the bits
in the V register.  GA ZERO is asserted if all the address bits on
the G bus are zero.

	The EVAL FSM can read certain constants onto the E bus,
rather than reading the contents of a register.  This is done by
asserting EA LIT or ET LIT.  These respectively read (six 0's,
LIT0-LIT2, 0) onto EA0-EA9, and LIT0-LIT2 onto ET0-ET2.  In this way
any constant type can be specified, and a small range of EVEN
addresses. 

	The GC FSM can connect the busses in certain ways.  The
signal CONN I connects the input pads to the G bus; the input pads
thus serve as a "read-only register" to the G bus in much the same
way that the S register (to which the output pads are normally tied)
serves as a "write-only register".  The signal CONN EA connects the
address bits of the E and G busses together; similarly CONN ET
independentlt connects the type bits of the E and G busses.  When
they are so tied, only one of EVAL and GC should be attempting to
read something onto its bus; the other may then load one or more
registers from this source.  Thus this bus connection facility
provides for bidirectional communication between the two sets of
registers; only one direction may be used per EVAL step, however.

                              SIMPLE
                        Tentative Microcode
                         Guy L. Steele Jr.
                          9 November 1978

	Here we present proposed microcode for the two FSMs.
Remember, in reading it, that the two machines are clocked in
parallel, but EVAL advances only when stepped by the STEP EVAL
signal from GC.

	The basic protocol is that GC is in a loop, constantly
performing GCOP DISP according to the operation GCOP requested by
the current EVAL microcode word.  When GC has performed the
requested operation, it steps EVAL and then returns to its loop.

	There are basically five kinds of operation EVAL can
request:  NOP, CAR/CDR, CONS/XCONS, RPLACD, and load/store Q.  NOP
means do nothing; it is used when EVAL just wants to shuffle things
on the E bus.  GC takes two cycles to perform a NOP: one to dispatch
on the GCOP, and one to STEP EVAL.  (The synchrony of the two FSMs
prevents these two operations from being simultaneous.)  Thus EVAL
proceeds at at most half the speed of GC.

	CAR/CDR operations request GC to perform a CAR or CDR
operation on memory.  Many variants of this are provided to optimize
data shuffling between the E and G busses.  A general convention is
that after a CAR/CDR operation the result of the operation is left
in Q, and the original operand is left in R.  The basic CAR and CDR
operations take their operand from the E bus; the result is left in
Q.  The CDRQ, CARQ, CARR variants take their operands from the
indicated register, and return the result to the E bus (and also put
it in Q).  The CDRRX, CARRX, CDRQX variants take their operands from
the indicated register, put the result in Q, and make no attempt to
use the E bus.  In this way EVAL can request GC to do something
useful while simultaneously doing something else with the E bus.

	The CONS/XCONS operations accept a CAR/CDR pointer from the
E bus, and take the contents of Q to be the CDR/CAR pointer, and
then allocate a new two-word cell.  A pointer to the result, with
type 0 (list), is left in Q.

	The RPLACDR operation alters the CDR of the cell pointed to
by R to be what is passed from the E bus.

	The load/store Q operations allow EVAL to access the Q
register.  RDQ requests GC to pass the contents of Q back to the E
bus.  RDQA asks for just the address bits; EVAL typically supplies
the type bits from a literal using ET LIT.  LDQ stuffs the E bus
contents into Q.  RDQCDRRX is a combination of RDQ and CDRRX useful
in odd circumstances.

	The initialization is a bit peculiar.  EVAL begins at state
INIT and GC at state GC.  The first thing EVAL does is put a pointer
in register C with type 2.  This 2 is also gated into the address
bits, providing a 4 there.  The 4 is irrelevant to the value placed
in C, but is needed by GC, which will connect the E and G busses on
that cycle.  This 4 is used to fatch the initial free storage
pointer, and is also placed in Q so that EVAL can later request CARQ
to get the expression to be evaluated.  EVAL also initializes
register N to contain NIL (a zero type/zero address pointer).

	Register C always contains a control stack for the recursive
evaluation.  Quantities which need to be saved are consed onto this
stack.  The CDR pointers which chain the stack cells together are
usually of type 0 (list), but sometimes have other type codes which
encode "return addresses" within the EVAL microcode.  At the state
labelled POPJ, the EVAL FSM dispatches on the type code of what is
in C.  This specifies what to do next on a return from a "recursive
call" to EVAL.  The type 2 pointer which is initially placed in C
specifies that EVAL should "drop dead", as the expression evaluation
has been completed.  (The current microcode does not show this, but
maybe EVAL shoudld arrange to write the result pointer into some
convenient place like location 6?)

	The microcode is written in a "LISPy" form, as a list of
microwords.  A symbol is a tag denoting the address of the following
microinstruction..  A list is a set of signals forming a single
microcode word.  In general, a signal like LDC is asserted iff its
name is present in the microinstruction.  Multi-bit fields are
specified by a list of the field name and the value.  LIT is
specified numerically, but GCOP is specified symbolically.  The
NSTATE and OSTATE fields are assumed to be handled specially by the
microassembler.  OSTATE is just the address of the current state.
NSTATE may be explicitly specified by (GOTO <tag>), or implicitly
specified by the rule that omitting a (GOTO <tag>) means that the
next state is the TEXTUALLY following microinstruction.  (This does
NOT imply that the microinstructions have consecutive "addresses",
but only that one has the address of another in its NSTATE field.)
The address of a microinstruction may be constrained by an "="
specifier (this idea is borrowed from the microassembler used by DEC
for KL10 microcode).  A specification such as "(= 1 * *)" means that
the addresses of the next four (2 to the power <number of *'s>)
microinstructions are constrained to end in 100, 101, 110, 111 in
that order.  Thus an explicit 1 or 0 forces an address bit to be
that, while a * indicates that either may be true, and all
combinations are used in lexicographic order for textually
successive instructions.  After the 2-to-the-power-<number-of-*'s>
instructions, one writes "(=)" to mark the end of the table;  this
is used for error-checking by the microassembler.  This
address-alignment facility is used for aligning dispatch tables.

(SETQ EVALCODE
      '(INIT (ET-LIT EA-LIT (LIT 2) LDC)
	     (EA-LIT ET-LIT (LIT 0) LDN)
	     ((GCOP CARQ) LDX ET-DISP (GOTO EVAL))
	EVAL
	(= * * *)
	     (RDX LDV (GOTO POPJ))			;LIST
	     (RDX LDV (GOTO POPJ))			;SYMBOL
	     ((GCOP LDQ) RDN (GOTO VAR1))		;VARIABLE
	     (RDX LDV (GOTO POPJ))			;CLOSURE
	     ((GCOP CDR) RDX (GOTO PROC1))		;PROCEDURE
	     ((GCOP CDR) RDX (GOTO IF1))		;IF
	     (EA-LIT ET-LIT (LIT 0) LDL (GOTO EVARGS))	;COMBINATION
	     ((GCOP CDR) RDX (GOTO QUOTE1))		;QUOTE
	(=)
	VAR1
	     (RDX+ LDV EAZ-ETZ-EQV-DISP)
	(= * 1 1)					;EA ZERO DISP
	     ((GCOP CDRQX) RDV LDX (GOTO VAR1))		;NONZERO
	     ((GCOP CARQ) LDV (GOTO POPJ))		;ZERO
	(=)
	PROC1
	     ((GCOP XCONS) RDN)
	     ((GCOP RDQA) ET-LIT (LIT 3) LDV (GOTO POPJ))
	QUOTE1
	     ((GCOP RDQ) LDV (GOTO POPJ))
	IF1
	     ((GCOP XCONS) RDC)
	     ((GCOP CONS) RDN)
	     ((GCOP RDQA) ET-LIT (LIT 1) LDC)		;IF2
	     ((GCOP CAR) RDX)
	     ((GCOP RDQ) LDX ET-DISP (GOTO EVAL))
	POPJ
	     ((GCOP CAR) RDC ET-DISP)
	(= 1 * *)					;ONLY ET1-ET2 RELEVANT
	     ((GCOP CONS) RDV (GOTO EV3))		;EV2
	     ((GCOP RDQCDRRX) LDN (GOTO IF3))		;IF2
	DEAD
	     ((GOTO DEAD))				;TOP LEVEL
	     ((GOTO DEAD))				;???
	(=)
	IF3
	     ((GCOP CDRQ) LDC)
	     ((GCOP CARRX) RDV EAZ-ETZ-EQV-DISP)
	IF4
	(= * 1 1)					;EA ZERO DISP
	     ((GCOP CARQ) LDX ET-DISP (GOTO EVAL))	;NONZERO
	     ((GCOP CDRQX) (GOTO IF4))			;ZERO
	(=)

	EV1
	     ((GCOP XCONS) RDC)
	     ((GCOP CONS) RDN)
	     ((GCOP CONS) RDL)
	     ((GCOP RDQA) ET-LIT (LIT 0) LDC)		;EV2
	     ((GCOP CAR) RDX)
	     ((GCOP RDQ) LDX ET-DISP (GOTO EVAL))
	EV3
	     ((GCOP RDQ) LDL)
	     ((GCOP CDR) RDC)
	     ((GCOP CARQ) LDN)
	     ((GCOP CDRRX))
	     ((GCOP CDRQ) LDC)
	     ((GCOP CARR) LDX ET-DISP (GOTO EVARGS))
	EVARGS
	(= * * *)
	     ((GCOP CDR) RDX (GOTO EV1))		;MORE
	     ((GCOP CAR) RDV (GOTO STOREV))		;CAR
	     ((GCOP CDR) RDV (GOTO STOREV))		;CDR
	     ((GCOP CDR) RDL (GOTO CONS1))		;CONS
	     (RDV EAZ-ETZ-EQV-DISP (GOTO ATOM1))	;ATOM
	     ((GCOP CDR) RDL (GOTO EQ1))		;EQ
	     (RDL LDV (GOTO POPJ))			;LIST
	     ((GCOP CDR) RDV (GOTO FUN1))		;FUNCALL
	(=)
	CONS1
	     ((GCOP CARQ))
	     ((GCOP XCONS) RDV)
	STOREV
	     ((GCOP RDQ) LDV (GOTO POPJ))		;PUT Q INTO V, POPJ
	EQ1
	     ((GCOP CARQ) EAZ-ETZ-EQV-DISP)
	(= 1 1 *)					;EQV DISP
	     (EA-LIT ET-LIT (LIT 0) LDV (GOTO POPJ))	;NOT EQ
	     (EA-LIT ET-LIT (LIT 1) LDV (GOTO POPJ))	;EQ
	(=)
	ATOM1
	(= 1 * 1)					;ET ZERO DISP
	     (EA-LIT ET-LIT (LIT 1) LDV (GOTO POPJ))	;ATOM
	     (EA-LIT ET-LIT (LIT 0) LDV (GOTO POPJ))	;LIST
	(=)
	FUN1
	     ((GCOP RDQ) LDN)
	     ((GCOP CDR) RDL EAZ-ETZ-EQV-DISP)
	FUN2
	(= * 1 1)					;EA ZERO DISP
	     ((GCOP CDRQ) EAZ-ETZ-EQV-DISP (GOTO FUN2))	;NONZERO
	     ((GCOP RPLACDR) RDN (GOTO FUN3))		;ZERO
	(=)
	FUN3
	     (RDL LDN)
	     ((GCOP CAR) RDV)
	     ((GCOP RDQ) LDX ET-DISP (GOTO EVAL))))

(SETQ GCCODE
      '(GC (CONN-EA LDS LDQ ADR)			;EVAL SUPPLIES 4
	   (ADR)
	   (ADR CONN-I LDP STEP-EVAL (GOTO LOOP))	;READ INITIAL FREE PTR
	LOOP
	   (GCOP-DISP)
	(= * * * *)
	   (STEP-EVAL (GOTO LOOP))			;NOP
	   (CONN-EA CONN-ET LDS LDR ADR (GOTO CDR1))	;CDR
	   (RDP LDQ (GOTO CAR1))			;CAR
	   (RDQ LDS LDR ADR (GOTO CDRQ1))		;CDRQ
	CARQ0 (RDP LDR (GOTO CARQ1))			;CARQ
	   (RDR LDQ (GOTO CARQ0))			;CARR
	   (RDR LDS ADR (GOTO CDR1))			;CDRRX
	   (RDP LDQ (GOTO CARRX1))			;CARRX
	   (RDQ LDS LDR ADR (GOTO CDR1))		;CDRQX
	   (RDP+ LDS LDR ADR GA-ZERO-DISP (GOTO CONS1))	;CONS
	   (RDP+ LDS LDR ADR GA-ZERO-DISP (GOTO XCONS1))	;XCONS
	   (RDR LDS ADRR (GOTO RPLACDR1))		;RPLACDR
	   (CONN-EA CONN-ET LDQ STEP-EVAL (GOTO LOOP))	;LDQ
	   (CONN-EA CONN-ET RDQ STEP-EVAL (GOTO LOOP))	;RDQ
	   (CONN-EA RDQ STEP-EVAL (GOTO LOOP))		;RDQA
	   (RDR LDS ADR (GOTO RDQCDRRX1))		;RDQCDRRX
	(=)
	CDR1
	   (ADR (GOTO CAR3))
	CAR1
	   (CONN-EA CONN-ET LDP LDR)
	CAR2
	   (RDP+ LDS ADR)
	   (ADR RDQ LDP)
	CAR3
	   (ADR CONN-I LDQ STEP-EVAL (GOTO LOOP))
	CARQ1
	   (RDQ LDP)
	   (RDP+ LDS ADR)
	   (ADR RDR LDP)
	   (ADR RDQ LDR)
	   (CONN-I CONN-EA CONN-ET LDQ STEP-EVAL (GOTO LOOP))
	CARRX1
	   (RDR LDP (GOTO CAR2))

	RPLACDR1
	   (ADR)
	   (ADR)
	   (CONN-EA CONN-ET LDS LDQ)
	   (WRITE)
	   (WRITE (GOTO CONS6))
	XCONS1
	(= *)
	   (ADR RDR LDP (GOTO XCONS2))
	GCDEAD (GOTO GCDEAD)				;FREE PTR WRAPPED AROUND
	(=)
	XCONS2
	   (ADR RDQ LDR)
	   (CONN-EA CONN-ET LDS (GOTO CONS3))
	CONS1
	(= *)
	   (ADR RDR LDP (GOTO CONS2))
	   (GOTO GCDEAD)
	(=)
	CONS2
	   (ADR CONN-EA CONN-ET LDR)
	   (RDQ LDS)
	CONS3
	   (WRITE RDP LDQ)
	   (WRITE)
	   (WRITE)
	   (RDP+ LDS)				;LEAVE GAP BETWEEN WRITE AND ADR
	   (ADR)
	   (ADR)
	   (ADR)
	   (RDR LDS)
	   (WRITE RDP+ LDR)
	   (WRITE RDR LDP)
	CONS6
	   (WRITE STEP-EVAL (GOTO LOOP))
	RDQCDRRX1
	   (ADR RDQ LDR)
	   (ADR CONN-I LDQ)
	   (RDR STEP-EVAL (GOTO LOOP))))

                              SIMPLE
               Circuit Diagrams and Tentative Layout
                         Guy L. Steele Jr.
                          9 November 1978

	The accompanying drawings constitute a complete proposed
circuit diagram for SIMPLE.  They also indicate an approximate
layout strategy.  There are one omissions and three changes.  The
routing of clock signals and power is not completely shown.  The
"probe muxes" are not planned to be in the positions shown, but will
lie horizontally just above the PLAs where the signals emerge; they
were drawn as they were to avoid cluttering (!) the diagrams.  The
pad layout ought to be changed, putting some pads along the top.
The probe control pads PC1-PC5 should be replaced by three
binary-encoded pads PC0-PC2.

	The main diagram shows the planned general layout of the
chip.  This is broken into five general areas, for each of which
there is an expanded diagram.  These diagrams in turn make use of
cells (such as register bits) which are individually documented. 

	I have shown the inputs IT0-IT2, IA0-IA9 as being connected
to super-buffers in order to reduce the apparent capacitance to the
outside world.  When GC connects both I and E to the G bus, the I
signals can have to drive three long busses and one or more
registers.  Are these superbuffers indeed a good idea in this
context?

	Also, I have shown the use of clocked drivers consisting of
an inverter and an inverting superbuffer for driving register
signals gated by PHI1.  The gating of PHI1 is to allow for possible
pre-charging of the busses (is this a good idea?).  There is one
driver for each register of thirteen bits.  Hence each driver is
planned to drive thirteen min-sized gates down a poly wire.  The
PHI1 signal will be routed to the clocked drivers on metal, not
poly.  Buffering of PHI2 has not been shown in these diagrams.
(Basically, I'm still a little in the dark at this point about the
details of clock routing, such as how much buffering is needed and
how much power the clock lines need to be able to carry.)

                              SIMPLE
                           Area Estimate
                         Guy L. Steele Jr.
                          9 November 1978

	Each FSM has about 60 states.  At 7 lambda per state, plus
about 100 lambda for pullups and in/out gating, that comes to 520
lambda for the height of the PLAs.  I think each register bit can be
kept down to about 35 lambda in height (counting 7 lambda for
running each of VDD, GND, E BUS, and I horizontally in metal, plus
an additional 7 lambda for the metal in the inverters in the
registers).  This times 13 bits per register comes to 455 lambda.
Pads at the upper edge contribute about 130 lambda.  The probe
multiplexor will be perhaps 70 lambda, and the random logic for
state multiplexing perhaps 50 lambda (?).  This adds up to
520+455+130+120=1220 lambda, or 3.6 mm in height.  Note that running
15 pads along each side requires 15*80=1200 lambda, so this works
out about right.

	As for width, the two PLAs together have 13 input bits and
64 output bits.  Each input bit takes 14 lambda and each output bit
7 lambda.  This gives a minimum of 602 lambda.  The pads along
either side contribute 260 lambda.  The minimum width is therefore
602+260=862 lambda, or 2.6 mm in width.  If we allow that the
complicated register cells take double the width of a simple
register cell, and allow two cell widths for the probe lines, two
for the input lines I routed down, and two for the EA LIT, ET LIT,
CONN I, CONN EA, and CONN ET gating, and two for the input
superbuffers and EA ZERO/ET ZERO lines, we get 20 register cell
widths which must fit along the E and G busses.  The space available
within the PLA width estimate is about 600 lambda.  This gives 30
lambda as the width of a register cell.  This may be a little tight.
We'll see.

	The overall area estimate is thus something like maybe 3.7
mm high by 2.7 mm wide.

                              SIMPLE
                    A Few Remarks on the Future
                         Guy L. Steele Jr.
                          9 November 1978

	You may have noticed that there are a few glaring omissions,
deficiencies, and loose ends in the design.  Please tell me about them!
But first, here are a few remarks about some things I know I have
left out, and inelegancies due to limited area.

	I regard SIMPLE as "merely" a prototype for some larger chip
to be designed in the rosy future, called, say, just "A Micro-
Processor for LISP Expressions" (AMPLE???).  AMPLE should provide
ample pointer-crunching power for real applications.  SIMPLE is
intended to provide a simple testbed for only a few of the ideas.
AMPLE would include many more features and be a complete working
"LISP" system.

	An inelegancy, for example, is that a special type "symbol"
is provided and nothing is done with it.  In SIMPLE, a symbol is
just an atomic list (?!).  I suppose one might use its CAR for a
global value cell and its CDR for a property list.

	Also, a conditional expression need not occupy three
word-pairs.  I did it that way to simplify the microcode in my mind.
I may change my mind again tomorrow.

	One extremely glaring omission in SIMPLE is that GC is not
really a garbage collector, but only a storage allocator.  once
you have consed memory once, that's it.  (This mode was once
used on the 7090: "How could anyone write a program so complicated
that it had to CONS 32K of memory??"  But how soon we learned!)

	Another omission is that there is no way to do RPLACA, and
the RPLACD operation is not made available to the user -- it is used
only to perform an NCONC when augmenting the environment during a
FUNCALL operation.  More generally, many nice primitive operators
such as NULL and MEMQ were omitted.  (Note, however, that the
apparently complicated operator LIST was included.  This is actually
the simplest of the lot, for when it is to be performed a list of
the arguments is already available!  There is a slight
inconsistency, however, and I am not sure whether it is worth
fixing.  In order to preserve the left-to-right order of evaluation
(which in SIMPLE you cannot detect anyway because there are no
side-effct operators...), the arguments in a procedure call shoudl
appear in order.  This means that the list of values is in reverse
order.  The CONS operator accounts for this, but this implies that
the LIST operator is actually a REVERSE-LIST operator.  C'est la vie.)

	The method of allowing recursion by passing an applied
closure as one of its "arguments" was swiped from Backus' Turing
Lecture (see CACM).  This permits the omission of SCHEME's LABELS
operator, which is fairly complicated to implement.  It provides the
power of LISP's LABEL operator, which will suffice for SIMPLE.
AMPLE will probably want LABEL in all its glory.  It may also want
other special forms, such as COND and CATCH (as in SCHEME).

	Note that no explicit iteration constant is provided.  It
can be obtained by using recursion.  The evaluation mechanism, like
SCHEME's, is "tail-recursive"; thus obviously iterative algorithms
expressed recursively do in fact behave iteratively.  Unfortunately
for SIMPLE, while the control stack does not grow unboundedly during
such an iteration, many cells may be consed onto the stack and then
thrown away again.  Only a garbage collector will solve this
problem. 

	In AMPLE the word size will probably be much larger -- say
five bits of type and perhaps thirty-two of address.  (These
numbers, like those in SIMPLE, are carefully chosen to make the word
size and number of output pins prime.)  Some attention to this has
been paid in the design of SIMPLE -- this is why each register has
its own signal driver superbuffers, etc.  One thing I did not do in
SIMPLE is use tri-state pads for the memory data connections.
Tri-state pads will be an absolute necessity for a 37-bit-word
AMPLE!  I did not use such pads in SIMPLE because they were not in
the cell library and I had no time to design them.  I would
appreciate any other comments about how scaling to a word size three
times greater may have to affect the design.
 